                  <provenance type="#e"><orgName role="fmo" key="mlgbOrg_426">Oxford, Oxfordshire.
                        Balliol College (Domus de Balliolo)</orgName><p>Mynors, Balliol, 195–196
                        transcribes many notes, verses, and marks, some early.‘ On 1v is: ¶ Summa
                        xxx d. ¶ Notule Sentenciarum || erasure || ¶ Ani a bello de Lewys xliii | ¶
                        Ani a bello de Ewysham xlii (indicating A.D. 1307) [...] On 3v in a long
                        erasure: . . . prec’ . . . fratri Thome G (?) . . . liber . . . usualis (the
                        last word might be custodialis, which would indicate Franciscan ownership).
                        Probably next in order of date is (295) Liber Willelmi Marche capellani.
                        Then (3v): Iste liber constat fratri Johanni Kekelly | ex dono fratris
                        Johannis Mnscham’ (Mynors, Balliol, 196)‘Liber domus de Balliolo ex dono |
                        Magistri Roberti Roke’ (fol. 3v)</p></provenance>
                  <provenance type="MLGB3_laterOwners">Robert Rooke (Roke) alias Cledeshow (d.
                     1458), fellow of Balliol</provenance>
